{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779125310,"nonce":"1dFUytNsRUcuNZqecIpjtIjlf/4=","title":"Studio: task-aware training metrics from analysis specs","message":"## Problem\n\nThe first Train/Evaluate collection UI slice (`f0ae3ce`) hardcoded example metrics such as velocity RMSE, peak velocity, and hold drift into the run table because the seeded RLRMP example already had those values. That is useful as a review fixture, but it is not a general product model.\n\nMetrics are task- and protocol-dependent. Velocity RMSE between replicates may make sense for a center-out reaching task, but it may be irrelevant or misleading for stabilization tasks or other tasks where velocity has different semantics. Hold drift and peak velocity are similarly task-specific. Studio should not bake these columns into the Train table as universal fields.\n\n## Desired Model\n\nTraining/evaluation tables should have configurable metric columns whose definitions come from typed specs, templates, or user-authored analysis/objective terms.\n\nA plausible interaction model:\n\n- The user defines or selects metrics in the structural/analysis authoring surface: state selectors, task objects, temporal windows, aggregation level, units, and whether lower/higher is better.\n- Some tasks/templates provide sensible default metrics, such as final position error for reaching, hold drift for hold phases, energy/effort, success rate, or task-specific stability metrics.\n- A user can mark an analysis output or metric spec as \"show during training\" or \"show in run table\".\n- Metrics can run during training/evaluation if the backend supports streaming or periodic validation, then update table columns live.\n- Metric columns expose sort direction and semantics rather than assuming every value is minimized.\n- The table can still show loss by default because loss is always part of training, but non-loss metrics should be attached to task/protocol/analysis specs.\n\n## UI Implications\n\n- Run tables should render columns from metric specs, not hardcoded RLRMP fields.\n- Column headers should be sortable; \"best\" should usually be a sorted view rather than a separate summary card.\n- Metric provenance and definitions should be inspectable from the column header or row detail, not repeated as raw metadata.\n- Imported legacy runs may have values for metrics without full metric specs; the UI should label those as imported metrics until they are mapped to typed specs.\n\n## Related Surfaces\n\n- `f0ae3ce` — current Train/Evaluate collection UI slice that exposed the problem.\n- `5378a5d` — structural/scenario authoring workspace; metrics should be authored as scenario/objective/analysis state.\n- `0c46f69` — pipeline workspace; metric columns flow through run/evaluation/analysis/report stages.\n- `0e1cd8c` — probe/metric nodes as loss targets.\n- `40f8322` — multi-run loss/metric comparison plotting.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}